  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The study relies on cross-sectional survey data to capture residents' perceptions, which limits causal claims about how neighborhood atmosphere alters attitudes over time.",
      "what": "This study evaluated the relationship between rising housing vacancy and public facility allocation across shrinking cities in Northeast China, examining public administration, commercial services, and facility diversity through physical spatial metrics and resident surveys. The findings demonstrate that facility diversity is particularly responsive to housing vacancy rates, with severely vacant cities experiencing larger shifts in facility distribution and stronger negative impacts on resident satisfaction. Additionally, local neighborhood atmosphere was found to moderate how residents perceive the link between vacant housing and nearby commercial services.",
      "why": "It provides empirical evidence on how urban shrinkage alters specific categories of municipal services, demonstrating that facility diversity declines more sharply than individual service types as housing vacancy worsens."
    }
